WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:02.032
STEPHANIE WONG: The
flexibility of Kubeflow

00:00:02.032 --> 00:00:05.190
lets you run your ML jobs
anywhere you can run Kubernetes

00:00:05.190 --> 00:00:07.920
and gives you an entire
platform to work with.

00:00:07.920 --> 00:00:11.160
Sometimes you don't need the
entire Kubeflow platform,

00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:13.500
but you still want to use
pipelines to orchestrate

00:00:13.500 --> 00:00:15.090
machine learning workflows.

00:00:15.090 --> 00:00:18.330
Let's look at Cloud AI Platform
Pipelines, a Google Cloud

00:00:18.330 --> 00:00:21.210
service that lets you
focus even more on your ML

00:00:21.210 --> 00:00:22.590
and less on your infrastructure.

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:26.027
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:26.763 --> 00:00:28.180
If you want to
read more about how

00:00:28.180 --> 00:00:29.860
to run Pipelines
on Google Cloud,

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.990
check out the
documentation link below.

00:00:31.990 --> 00:00:34.930
Previously, I talked about how
to work with Pipelines as part

00:00:34.930 --> 00:00:38.530
of a Kubeflow deployment, but
we can use Cloud AI Platform

00:00:38.530 --> 00:00:40.780
Pipelines to get the
same user interface

00:00:40.780 --> 00:00:42.587
and back-end functionality.

00:00:42.587 --> 00:00:44.170
If you don't need
the rest of Kubeflow

00:00:44.170 --> 00:00:46.930
and are just looking for the
ability to work with pipelines,

00:00:46.930 --> 00:00:48.940
Cloud AI Platform
gives you a quick way

00:00:48.940 --> 00:00:50.770
to set up an installation.

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:53.290
On top of that, authentication
with other Google Cloud

00:00:53.290 --> 00:00:55.030
services is built in.

00:00:55.030 --> 00:00:57.010
This lets you focus on
building your pipeline

00:00:57.010 --> 00:00:59.230
and integrating with
other Cloud services

00:00:59.230 --> 00:01:02.540
without needing to manage
a complex deployment.

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.860
Let's start by getting a
Pipelines installation set up.

00:01:05.860 --> 00:01:08.410
To try it out, head to
the Google Cloud console

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.590
and use the left navigation
to find Pipelines

00:01:11.590 --> 00:01:13.430
under AI Platform.

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:16.150
Then click the New
Instance button.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:18.310
Google Cloud will set
up a Kubernetes cluster

00:01:18.310 --> 00:01:21.280
and then deploy the Pipeline
installation on top of it,

00:01:21.280 --> 00:01:23.740
but all the details
are handled for you.

00:01:23.740 --> 00:01:25.600
Once it's set up
and running, you'll

00:01:25.600 --> 00:01:29.880
have the full Pipelines UI
and SDK to start working with.

00:01:29.880 --> 00:01:31.470
We'll go back to
the Pipelines page

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.810
and click Open Pipelines
Dashboard to get

00:01:33.810 --> 00:01:35.760
to the same user interface.

00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:38.790
We can see experiments and
runs here, as well as look

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:40.633
at our metadata and artifacts.

00:01:40.633 --> 00:01:43.050
Don't forget to check out the
previous metadata management

00:01:43.050 --> 00:01:46.620
episode, which goes into
more detail about it.

00:01:46.620 --> 00:01:49.140
So it's still possible to
upload a compiled pipeline

00:01:49.140 --> 00:01:51.570
through the same user interface,
but what if you're using

00:01:51.570 --> 00:01:53.290
a notebook for your model?

00:01:53.290 --> 00:01:56.100
Here's where we can use another
service to help us out--

00:01:56.100 --> 00:01:58.200
Cloud AI Platform Notebooks.

00:01:58.200 --> 00:01:59.700
In our previous
videos, we've talked

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.610
about the advantages of
running a notebook in the cloud

00:02:02.610 --> 00:02:04.710
rather than on
your local machine.

00:02:04.710 --> 00:02:06.630
Cloud AI Platform
Notebooks gives you

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:08.699
a simple way to do just that.

00:02:08.699 --> 00:02:10.979
You can create a new
notebook quickly and jump

00:02:10.979 --> 00:02:13.290
into a JupyterLab interface.

00:02:13.290 --> 00:02:14.700
Back in the Google
Cloud console,

00:02:14.700 --> 00:02:18.060
just click New Instance,
and choose an environment.

00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:20.680
Since we're working with
a TensorFlow 1 example,

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:24.360
we'll choose TensorFlow
1.1.5 without any GPUs

00:02:24.360 --> 00:02:25.968
and hit Create.

00:02:25.968 --> 00:02:27.510
Once again, all of
the infrastructure

00:02:27.510 --> 00:02:29.380
is taken care of
behind the scenes,

00:02:29.380 --> 00:02:32.340
and we're able to just
click Open JupyterLab to get

00:02:32.340 --> 00:02:35.340
to our interface, where we can
create notebooks and execute

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:36.210
them.

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:38.760
Since we're using the same
notebook from the last video,

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:42.080
we've already cloned the
same repository here.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.300
Code for Pipelines is
made to be reusable

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:46.070
so we don't need
to change anything

00:02:46.070 --> 00:02:47.870
to create an execution.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:51.230
We will need to specify the same
properties-- our Google Cloud

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:53.120
project and the Cloud
Storage bucket where

00:02:53.120 --> 00:02:54.620
our model will be stored.

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:56.570
There's one more
change we need to make.

00:02:56.570 --> 00:02:59.000
We have to tell the pipeline's
SDK where our pipeline's

00:02:59.000 --> 00:03:00.540
endpoint is located.

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:03.920
This is as easy as copying the
URL of our hosted pipeline,

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:07.010
which we can get by clicking
Settings next to a pipeline

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.470
and storing it as a
variable in our notebook.

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:12.230
Since the same user created both
the notebook and the pipeline's

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:15.730
installation, the authentication
is taken care of for us.

00:03:15.730 --> 00:03:18.230
We'll then modify the line
where we execute the pipeline

00:03:18.230 --> 00:03:21.440
and point it to the server
and then run the notebook.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:23.640
Other than that, this
is the same pipeline,

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:25.160
so we'll still be
using the Cloud AI

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.860
platform to do the training
and serving of the model.

00:03:27.860 --> 00:03:30.640
We're just doing it from
a different environment.

00:03:30.640 --> 00:03:32.598
Cloud AI platforms
can be an easy way

00:03:32.598 --> 00:03:34.890
to get a pipelines environment
without having to set up

00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:36.720
an entire Kubeflow deployment.

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:39.000
In addition, it's easy to
integrate with other Google

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.880
Cloud services to help
extend your ML projects,

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:44.730
like hosting notebooks,
model training, and serving.

00:03:44.730 --> 00:03:45.810
Try it out for yourself.

00:03:45.810 --> 00:03:49.460
[MUSIC PLAYING]

